.\" pathchk.1 -- maux's pathchk(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" env.1/man.1.
.TH PATHCHK 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
pathchk \- check pathnames for validity/portability
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathchk
[
.B \-p
] [
.B \-P
]
.I pathname
.IR .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pathchk
checks each
.I pathname
operand and writes a diagnostic to standard error for any that
fails. With no option, the limits are this tree's OWN (not the
host's): each component at most 15 characters (ProDOS's own
directory\-entry name_length field), the whole pathname at most
64 characters (MLI_PTH_MAXLN, inc/mli.equ \(em the same cap
.BR MKPATH ()
enforces for every program in this tree), and each component
must begin with a letter and otherwise hold only letters,
digits, or a period (ProDOS's own naming rule, case\-insensitive).
An intermediate component that does not exist is
.B not
an error; one that exists and is not itself a directory is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
Check portability instead: the POSIX portable character set
.RI ([A\-Za\-z0\-9._\-]), " " 14
characters per component, 255 for the whole pathname
.RI ( _POSIX_NAME_MAX / _POSIX_PATH_MAX ,
the XCU's own limits). No existence check is made under
.BR \-p .
.TP
.B \-P
In addition to whichever of the above is active, an empty
.I pathname
or a component beginning with
.B \-
is also a failure.
.SH FILES
.br
/usr/man/PATHCHK  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mkdir(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every
.I pathname
passed, 1 if any failed, 2 for a usage error.
.SH BUGS
The whole\-pathname length check under
.B \-p
is limited by an 8\-bit counter and so cannot actually observe
the difference between 255 and 256 bytes \(em not a concern on
a system whose own absolute pathname cap is 64.
